{"kind":"assert","text":"S1 preparation: late abrupt disposal removes a restored session row\n\nOne component probe against unchanged main 9d96cf25 reproduced an automatic cleanup mismatch. OAuthHttpLayers.abrupt retires a held opening without recording its disposal mode. When that opening returns, open rejects the stale layer and schedules closeAll after abrupt. The observed order was abrupt, wire.endLegacy, closeAll, wire.endLegacy, wire.closeAll, remove. The synthetic durable store retained zero rows instead of one. The actual child exit is 1. Late detach retained its row, abrupt after opening retained its row, and late graceful disposal removed its row as expected.\n\nThis result uses the production OAuthHttpLayers and McpHttp classes with explicit standing, Wire, and session-store stand-ins. It proves the component cleanup order only. HostedAgent checks its epoch before restore and installation, then reauthorizes before dispatch. The probe does not establish ingress reachability, live row loss, Native authority, real SQLite behavior, a provider result, or a deployed effect. A later explicit authorized retirement can validly remove rows. The requirement concerns the disposal intent of automatic stale-opening cleanup.\n\nAt S1 implementation, add the characterization: “abrupt disposal of a held OAuth opening rejects the late layer and retains its restored session rows.” The attached map names 15 existing witnesses and their boundaries. Existing source witnesses were inspected, not rerun. Its separate candidate concerns bearer retirement during a held installed-application opening. The named rotation witness covers an already-open transport. Confirm coverage before adding another case; the narrow source search does not establish global absence.\n\nThe first Node invocation refused an unsupported loader flag with exit 9 before any component case ran. One subsequent invocation used already-installed TSX 4.21.0 from scratch and completed the four controlled scenarios above. Both process groups have ended. Source, lockfile, and security inventory hashes stayed unchanged. No package installation, source or seal edit, gate, hosted action, or deployment occurred. The evidence also preserves the sealer's self-matching marker refusal and its correction without repeating a probe.\n\nThe manifest verifies 31 read-only public files in three units. Its SHA-256 is 9fd1b20c4e32fc44d49444e72f2f0108f4b77bb39af8cf36dd38b776e42ef585. This is read-only preparation under e39e7cef and plan17ed150d. It starts no implementation branch, promise, review, or S1 acceptance. Order8a78f701 remains: M4 through review first. Archive ruling request1e43e8f2 remains open; both M4 gates remain failed.","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}